\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Background}

Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems combine information retrieval with
language generation. This design addresses a limit of purely parametric large
language models (LLMs): the model's knowledge is fixed at training time and
cannot be updated without retraining or fine-tuning it. \citet{lewis2020rag}
introduced RAG by pairing a pretrained retriever with a pretrained
sequence-to-sequence generator, so the model can base its output on external,
updatable evidence rather than only on what it learned during training. A RAG
system takes the user query, retrieves relevant information from an external
knowledge base, typically a vector database, and passes that information to a
language model together with instructions for generating the final response.

The system prompt connects the intended behaviour of the system to its
observable output. The retrieved evidence varies with the query and the
knowledge base, but the system prompt is written on purpose: it specifies how
the model should behave, which instructions and constraints apply, and which
output format it should produce. Because it is written in natural language
rather than as a formal specification, its effect on behaviour is hard to
predict and hard to check in advance. Its influence on both intended behaviour
and actual output therefore has to be studied through controlled experiments.

\section{Motivation and Research Objective}

How a RAG system responds to its system prompt matters in practice. Whether the
system follows its instructions reliably decides whether its behaviour can be
trusted once deployed, especially when the same retrieval and generation
components are reused across many applications with only the system prompt
changing. The question is then \textit{how systematic changes in the system
prompt can be shown to alter the behaviour and outcomes of a RAG system.}

Isolating that effect is difficult. The influence of the system prompt overlaps
with the influence of the retrieved evidence, of the language model itself, and
of the exact wording of the instructions, and all of these have to be controlled
before any change in behaviour can be attributed to the prompt rather than to
something else. Small, seemingly harmless changes to the wording of an
instruction can cause large changes in model behaviour
\citep{salinas2024butterfly}, and a system prompt's authority over the model is
not guaranteed once the user turn works against it \citep{mu2025robustness}.
Most of that work varies the instruction the user supplies rather than the
standing system prompt. Section~\ref{sec:promptlit} draws out that distinction,
and this thesis is built to address it by treating the system prompt as a
controlled experimental variable.

The central research question is accordingly: \textit{To what extent do
systematic changes in the system prompt alter the behaviour and outcomes of a
RAG system when the user input, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and
underlying language model are held constant?} Answering it requires a formal
model of the RAG system precise enough to isolate the system prompt as a
controlled parameter, and an experimental design that holds every other source
of variation fixed. The rest of this thesis builds both, and builds them for the
standard single-shot retrieval-augmented architecture. That restriction is
deliberate. As Section~\ref{sec:advanced} argues, the single-shot architecture
is the configuration in which the system prompt reaches the answer along a
single path, so its effect is identifiable. Graph-structured and agentic
pipelines let the prompt shape retrieval as well, and an effect measured inside
them could not be decomposed into a prompt component and a routing component.
What this thesis establishes is therefore a controlled baseline for those richer
pipelines rather than a claim about them; extending the design to a
graph-structured or agentic setting is left to future work.

\section{Thesis Structure}

This thesis has seven chapters. Chapter 2 covers the background needed for the
rest of the work: it traces how retrieval-augmented approaches to language
generation developed and introduces the components of a RAG system used in later
chapters. Chapter 3 reviews the relevant literature in three parts, namely
prompt sensitivity in language models, retrieval-augmented generation
specifically, and adversarial instructions delivered through retrieved content,
and identifies the research gap that follows from them. Chapter 4 builds the
formal model of the RAG system used in this thesis. It takes the probabilistic
formulation of \citet{lewis2020rag} and extends it so that the system prompt
becomes an explicit variable of the generator, grounds the knowledge base in
established question-answer datasets, and defines what counts as a good answer.
Chapter 5 describes the experimental setup: how the corpus and query set are
assembled from those datasets, which conditions the system is run under, how
each part of that definition is measured, and which estimators are used for the
criterion defined in Chapter 4. Chapter 6 presents and discusses the results of
the experiments. Chapter 7 concludes the thesis by summarising the findings and
outlining directions for future work.

\section*{Notation}
\label{sec:notation}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Notation}

Throughout this thesis, the following notation is used. It follows the probabilistic formulation of \citet{lewis2020rag} for the system itself and the intervention calculus of \citet{pearl2009causality} for the causal statements, and every symbol used in the later chapters is listed here rather than introduced again where it first appears.

\subsection*{The system and its components}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $q \in \mathcal{Q}$ denotes a user query, drawn from the space of possible queries $\mathcal{Q}$, and $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ denotes the distribution over that space from which queries are drawn.
    \item $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\} \in \mathcal{D}$ denotes the knowledge base, where each $d_i$ is a document, $N$ is the number of documents, and $\mathcal{D}$ is the space of possible knowledge bases.
    \item $S \in \mathcal{S}$ denotes a system prompt, drawn from the space of possible system prompts $\mathcal{S}$. It is the standing instruction supplied in the system role, fixed across requests, and is distinct from the query $q$, which the user supplies anew with each request.
    \item $C \in \mathcal{C}$ denotes the retrieved context supplied to the language model.
    \item $y = (y_1,\ldots,y_L) \in \mathcal{Y}$ denotes the generated system output, a sequence of $L$ tokens drawn from the space of possible responses $\mathcal{Y}$; $y_{1:i-1}$ denotes the tokens produced before position $i$.
    \item $R$ and $G$ denote the retrieval and generation functions of the pipeline, and $\chi$ the chunking strategy by which documents are divided into retrievable units.
    \item $v \in \mathcal{V}$ denotes a token of the vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, $u_v$ the score the generator assigns it, and $T > 0$ the decoding temperature.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{The probabilistic model}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $z \in D$ denotes a single retrieved document, and $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ the $K$ documents with the highest retrieval probability.
    \item $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$ denotes the retrieval distribution with parameters $\eta$, and $p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S)$ the generator with parameters $\theta$.
    \item $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ denotes the distribution over outputs obtained by marginalising the generator over the retrieved documents.
    \item $Y$ denotes the output viewed as a random variable, $M(Y)$ a measured property of it, and $\varepsilon$ the randomness introduced by decoding.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{The system prompt and its variation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\Sigma = \{\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m\}$ denotes the set of requirements a system prompt states, where each $\sigma_k$ is a property an answer either has or does not have, so that $\sigma_k(y) \in \{0,1\}$ is checkable on any output.
    \item $\varphi$ denotes the wording in which those requirements are expressed, and $S = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi)$ denotes the resulting system prompt.
    \item $S_{b,0},\ldots,S_{b,4}$ denote the five prompt conditions of baseline $b \in \{A,B,C\}$, fifteen in all, with $S_{b,0}$ the canonical form of that baseline. The first four share $\Sigma_b$ and differ in wording; the fifth is built from a modified requirement set $\Sigma_b'$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Causal intervention}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ denotes the intervention that sets the system prompt to condition $j$, as distinct from observing it to take that value.
    \item $\tau_j$ denotes the average causal effect of condition $S_j$ relative to the baseline $S_0$, taken over the query distribution $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$, and $\delta_j$ the interaction contrast between two evidence levels.
    \item $\mathbb{E}[\cdot]$ denotes expectation, and $\hat{\cdot}$ marks a quantity estimated from the sample rather than defined in the model.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Evidence and evaluation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\Phi$ denotes the facts the dataset annotations hold true, $\Phi_y$ the facts an answer asserts, $\Phi_C$ the facts contained in the retrieved context, and $a_q$ the reference answer annotated for query $q$.
    \item $\mathcal{Y}^{*}(q,C)$ denotes the set of acceptable answers, $n_k(y)$ the number of times an answer breaks requirement $k$, $\lambda_k$ the weight attached to that breach, and $\ell(y)$ the resulting cost of an answer.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Experimental design}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $c_{ik}$ denotes the $k$-th context given to query $q_i$, with $c^{\text{gold}}$, $c^{\text{ret}}$, $c^{\text{corr}}$ and $c^{\varnothing}$ the four evidence levels: annotated supporting passages, the genuine retriever output, corrupted passages, and no context at all.
    \item $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}}$ and $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}}$ denote the two query categories, and $N_Q$ the size of the query set.
    \item $\pi_i$ denotes the share of runs in which query $q_i$ is answered correctly with no context at all.
    \item $\hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j,c^{k})$ denotes the empirical risk, the average cost of the answers produced in one cell of the design, and $\hat{\tau}_{jk}$ the sample estimate of $\tau_j$ within evidence level $k$.
    \item The indices are $i \in \{1,\ldots,N_Q\}$ over queries, $j \in \{0,\ldots,4\}$ over prompt conditions, $k \in \{1,\ldots,4\}$ over evidence levels, and $r \in \{1,\ldots,n\}$ over the $n$ repetitions of each cell.
\end{itemize}

\chapter{Preliminaries}

Chapter 1 established that the system prompt is the interface through which a
RAG system's intended behaviour is specified, and asked how systematically
varying that interface changes system behaviour and outcomes. Answering that
question formally first requires the components of a RAG system and the reasons
behind its design. Section 2.1 traces how retrieval-augmented approaches to
language generation developed, Section 2.2 introduces the components used
throughout later chapters, and Section 2.3 discusses typical use cases and the
failure modes they expose.

\section{Path towards RAG}

The need to store knowledge outside of any single memory, and to retrieve it
again on demand, predates computing by a wide margin. Inca administrators
recorded census and tax data in knotted cords, quipu, that only specially
trained officials could encode or decode, which separated the record itself from
the person consulting it. Long before that, the Library of Alexandria's
cataloguer Callimachus organised its holdings by author and subject in the
Pinakes, one of the first attempts to make a large external collection
searchable rather than merely stored. \citet{bush1945asmaythink} made the need
for machine-assisted retrieval explicit and proposed a device that could store a
person's library and retrieve from it through associative links between related
items instead of one fixed order. \citet{codd1970relational} gave the idea a
rigorous computational form by treating a knowledge store as a formally
queryable structure. \citet{salton1975vectorspace} then extended retrieval from
exact structured queries to similarity ranking over unstructured text, the
direct ancestor of the embedding-based retrieval described later in this
chapter.

The same need, a mechanism to retrieve from an external store instead of
remembering everything internally, carried over into question-answering systems,
which did not begin with language models either. Early systems relied on
hand-crafted rules and pattern matching over the input question, and mapped
recognised syntactic patterns onto retrieval or answer-generation procedures
\citep{pandya2021qasurvey}. Statistical and later neural methods replaced those
rule-based components, and the dominant approach shifted toward learning the
mapping from question to answer directly from data. Large language models are
the end point of that shift: they generate answers from parameters learned over
large text corpora rather than from any explicit rule set.

Large language models generate text using only parametric knowledge, that is,
information encoded in their weights during training
\citep{wang2024knowledgemechanisms}. This has two problems. First, a model's
knowledge is fixed at the point its training data was collected. Any event,
fact, or document from after that point is unknown to the model unless it is
retrained or fine-tuned, and both options are costly and have to be repeated
every time knowledge changes. Second, purely parametric models are prone to
hallucination: they can generate fluent, plausible-sounding statements that no
evidence supports, because nothing in their design forces an output to trace
back to a verifiable source.

Both problems are the reason to give a language model access to an external,
updatable source of evidence at inference time, instead of relying only on what
is stored in its parameters \citep{lin2025llminference}. \citet{lewis2020rag}
formalised this idea as Retrieval-Augmented Generation. RAG combines a
pretrained neural retriever, which selects relevant passages from a document
collection, with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, which conditions
its output on those retrieved passages in addition to the input query. The same
language model can then be paired with different or updated document collections
without retraining, and a generated response can be grounded in specific
retrieved evidence and, in principle, traced back to it.

RAG has developed beyond that original design since it was introduced.
\citet{gao2023survey} describe three stages: Naive RAG, which runs retrieval and
generation as one fixed pipeline; Advanced RAG, which adds steps such as query
reformulation or re-ranking of retrieved passages before generation; and Modular
RAG, which makes retrieval, generation, and other steps separate, swappable
components that can be rearranged for different tasks. Retrieval and generation
stay separate steps in all three. This thesis therefore treats them as two
distinct functions, so their interaction with the system prompt can be studied
on its own, as formalised in Chapter 4.
\section{Components of RAG}

A RAG system has four components: the knowledge base the evidence comes from,
the retriever that selects relevant evidence for a given query, the generator
that produces a response from the query and the retrieved evidence, and the
system prompt that specifies how the generator should behave. This section
introduces each component in turn; Chapter 4 treats them formally.
Figure~\ref{fig:rag} shows how they fit together and which data passes between
them.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{\input{figures/rag_architecture.tex}}
\caption{Architecture of a RAG system, showing the four components introduced in this section. The retrieval stage matches the user query against a vector database and returns the top-$K$ chunks. The generation stage combines those chunks with the user query and the system prompt into a single prompt and produces the answer.}
\label{fig:rag}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Knowledge Base / Vector Database}

The knowledge base $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\}$ is the collection of documents
the evidence comes from. In most modern RAG systems, documents are first split
into smaller passages, called chunks. Chunking is needed because a language
model can only take in a limited amount of context, and retrieving a whole
document would add mostly irrelevant text along with the relevant part. The
chunk size is a trade-off: small chunks are precise but may separate a statement
from the sentence that qualifies it, while large chunks keep more context but
dilute the part that matches the query. \citet{chen2024densex} compare retrieval
units of different granularity and report that the choice changes both retrieval
quality and the quality of the final answer. Chunk size is therefore a design
parameter rather than an implementation detail.

An embedding model then maps each chunk to a high-dimensional vector, so that
chunks with similar meaning end up close together in the embedding space.
Representing text as vectors and measuring relatedness by geometric proximity
goes back to the vector space model of \citet{salton1975vectorspace}; with neural
embeddings, the vector is learned rather than derived from term counts.
Similarity between a query vector and a chunk vector is usually measured by the
cosine of the angle between them, which ignores vector length and compares
direction only.

These embeddings are stored in a vector database, which supports fast
nearest-neighbour search over large collections of chunks. Comparing a query
against every stored vector is exact but becomes too slow as the collection
grows, so vector databases index the vectors and use approximate
nearest-neighbour search, which trades a small loss in recall for a large gain
in speed. The most widely used index of this kind is the hierarchical navigable
small world graph of \citet{malkov2020hnsw}, which reaches a nearest neighbour by
descending through graph layers of decreasing coarseness instead of scanning the
collection.

Several systems build on such indexes. \citet{pan2024vectordb} survey how they
differ in indexing, query processing and storage; what matters here is how they
are deployed. FAISS is a library embedded directly in an application, with
fine-grained control over the index structure \citep{johnson2021faiss}. Chroma
is a lightweight database aimed at local development and small deployments. The
\texttt{pgvector} extension adds vector columns and similarity operators to
PostgreSQL, so vectors can sit next to existing relational data. Pinecone is a
managed service in which the provider handles indexing and scaling. All four
answer the same question, namely which stored vectors lie closest to a query
vector, so the choice affects latency, operational cost and reproducibility
rather than the logic of the pipeline. The indexing step as a whole, and in
particular the choice of chunk size and embedding model, decides what evidence
is even available to the retriever, regardless of how good the retriever itself
is.
\subsection{Retriever}

Given a user query $q$ and a knowledge base $D$, the retriever selects the chunks
that are relevant for answering $q$. Chapter 4 formalises this as a probability
distribution $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$ over candidate documents. Three approaches are
common, and they fail in different ways.
\subsubsection{Dense Retrieval}

Dense retrieval embeds the query with the same embedding model used for the
knowledge base and ranks chunks by the similarity between the query vector and
each chunk vector. Because the embedding is learned, two passages that express
the same content in different words end up close together, so a query can match
a chunk with which it shares no vocabulary. This is the mechanism the original
RAG architecture of \citet{lewis2020rag} relies on. The weakness follows from the
same property: rare identifiers, product codes, names and numbers carry little
semantic signal, and a dense retriever may rank a fluent but irrelevant passage
above the one chunk that contains the exact string the query asked about.

\subsubsection{Sparse Retrieval}

Sparse retrieval scores chunks by term overlap with the query. The standard
method is BM25 \citep{robertson2009bm25}, which weights each matching term by how
often it occurs in the chunk, how rare it is across the collection, and how long
the chunk is. Common words therefore contribute little, and long chunks are not
favoured merely for containing more words. The representation is sparse because
a chunk is described by the vocabulary it contains rather than by a dense
vector. BM25 requires no training and remains a strong baseline. It handles
exactly the cases dense retrieval struggles with, since it scores exact term
matches. It fails on paraphrase: a query and a chunk that share no terms receive
a score of zero regardless of how closely their meanings agree.
\subsubsection{Hybrid Search}

Since the two methods fail on different queries, many systems run both and merge
the results. The difficulty is that their scores are not on a common scale, so
they cannot simply be added. The usual solutions are to normalise the scores
before combining them with a weight, or to combine the rankings instead of the
scores. Reciprocal rank fusion \citep{cormack2009rrf} is the standard method of
the second kind: each chunk is scored by the reciprocal of its rank in each list,
so a chunk ranked highly by either method is promoted and the scores are never
compared directly. \citet{gao2023survey} describe hybrid retrieval as one of the
standard refinements that distinguish later RAG systems from the original design.

\subsubsection{Retrieval Depth}

Whichever method is used, the top-$K$ ranked chunks form the retrieved context.
Retrieval quality depends on both how many chunks are retrieved and how precise
the ranking is: too few chunks risk missing needed evidence, while too many risk
adding irrelevant or conflicting information to the generation step. The order of
the chunks also matters, since \citet{liu2024lostmiddle} report that answer
accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits within the context. This
thesis fixes the retriever, the chunking and $K$ across all experimental
conditions, so that changes in the output cannot be traced back to retrieval.

\subsection{Generator}
\label{sec:generator}

The generator is the large language model that produces the final response. Such
a model is a neural network trained on a large text corpus to predict the next
token in a sequence. It generates text by autoregressive decoding, producing one
token at a time, each conditioned on all previous tokens. Its parameters, written
$\theta$ in Chapter 4, encode what the model learned during training. This
encoded knowledge is often called parametric memory, and it is fixed once
training ends.

Within a RAG pipeline the model is used less as a store of facts than as a
reasoning and synthesis component conditioned on evidence supplied at inference
time: it reads the retrieved chunks, selects the parts that answer the query,
resolves references between them, and writes the result as fluent text. This
division is the point of the architecture. Facts come from the knowledge base,
which can be updated without retraining, while the model contributes language
competence and the ability to combine several passages into one answer
\citep{lewis2020rag}. When the model answers from parametric memory instead of
from the retrieved context, the answer may still be fluent and even correct, but
it is no longer grounded in evidence that can be checked. That is one route to
the unsupported claims documented by \citet{niu2024ragtruth}.

In practice the query, the retrieved context and the system prompt are not passed
to the model separately: a fixed template combines them into a single input
sequence, usually with the system prompt first so that it shapes how everything
after it is read. Chapter 4 formalises the generator as
$p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S)$, the probability of the next token given the
query, a retrieved document, the tokens already produced and the system prompt.
Because these three together determine the model's input, changing any one of
them can change the output. The others must therefore be held fixed if a change
in behaviour is to be attributed to the system prompt alone.

\subsubsection{Decoding and the Temperature Parameter}

Generation is not deterministic by default. At each step the model produces a
score $u_v$ for every token $v$ in the vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, and the softmax
function, with a temperature parameter $T > 0$, turns these scores into a
probability distribution:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:temperature}
p(v) = \frac{\exp(u_v / T)}{\sum_{v' \in \mathcal{V}} \exp(u_{v'} / T)}.
\end{equation}

The temperature rescales the scores before they are normalised, and it controls
how much probability mass is left for tokens the model considers less likely
\citep{holtzman2020degeneration}. As $T$ approaches zero the distribution
concentrates on the highest-scoring token, and decoding becomes effectively
deterministic: the same input produces the same output. As $T$ grows the
distribution flattens, lower-scoring tokens become reachable, and the output
becomes more varied across runs.

For open-ended writing this variety is useful. For a RAG system it is mostly a
liability. A token that the model scores as unlikely given the retrieved context
is, by construction, one that the evidence does not support well, so raising the
temperature increases the chance of producing a statement the retrieved documents
do not contain. High temperature also makes the system harder to evaluate, since
the same query and the same evidence no longer yield the same answer. RAG systems
intended to be faithful to their sources are therefore usually run at a low
temperature, trading variety for grounding and reproducibility. This thesis holds
$T$ fixed and low across all experimental conditions; the randomness that remains
at a fixed temperature is the reason each condition is repeated several times in
Chapter 5.
\subsection{System Prompt}

The system prompt $S$ specifies the instructions, constraints and output
requirements the generator should follow, independent of the query or retrieved
context. The query changes with every request, and the retrieved context changes
with the query and the knowledge base; the system prompt is usually fixed across
many requests.

One terminological point has to be settled here, because Chapter 3 reviews a
literature that does not settle it. In this thesis \emph{system prompt} denotes
$S$ alone: the standing instruction supplied in the system role by whoever
deploys the system. Much published work on prompt sensitivity uses \emph{prompt}
more broadly, for the entire input the model receives, or more narrowly, for the
task instruction and any worked examples accompanying the question in the user
turn. Under either reading the text that varies is what this thesis calls the
query $q$, or the query together with its framing, and not $S$. The two differ in
who writes the text and how often it changes: $q$ arrives with each request from
whoever is using the system, whereas $S$ is written once and applies to every
request thereafter. A finding about the one does not automatically carry over to
the other, and Chapter 3 keeps the two bodies of work apart for that reason.

Its role in the architecture is easiest to see by asking what happens without it.
Retrieval places relevant evidence in the context window, but placing evidence
there does not oblige the model to use it. Section~\ref{sec:generator} described
the parameters $\theta$ as a store of information absorbed during training, and
that store remains available at inference time. A model given a retrieved passage
and a question it believes it already knows the answer to may answer from
parameters rather than from the passage, producing an assured and fluent response
that the retrieved documents do not support. Nothing signals this failure to the
user: fluency and confidence do not depend on whether the underlying claim was
grounded. \citet{niu2024ragtruth} document that unsupported statements continue
to appear even when relevant evidence has been retrieved and supplied. The system
prompt is the last point in the pipeline at which this can be constrained. It is
the only component that can state, before generation begins, that the retrieved
context is the sole admissible source of fact.

The system prompt accordingly has two functions. The first is to define the
operational scope of the system: the role it occupies, the audience it addresses,
the register it writes in, and the format its output takes. The second is to
install hard constraints on that behaviour, of which the grounding constraint is
the most consequential. A grounding constraint restricts the model to the
retrieved context and specifies what to do when that context is inadequate. That
converts an unanswerable query from a case the model improvises around into one
with a defined response. The two functions differ in kind: scope shapes how an
answer is written, whereas a hard constraint determines whether an answer may be
given at all. Listing~\ref{lst:prompt} illustrates both in a minimal example.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={A minimal system prompt combining an operational scope with two hard constraints.},label={lst:prompt}]
You are a customer service assistant for a parcel delivery company.

GROUNDING RULE
Answer only from the context provided below. Do not use information
from your training data, even where you are confident that it is
correct.

INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE
If the context does not contain the answer, reply with exactly:
The provided documents do not contain this information.

FORMAT
Answer in at most three sentences. Cite the identifier of every
document you use, in square brackets.
\end{lstlisting}

The first line fixes the operational scope. The two blocks that follow are hard
constraints: one restricts the admissible source of fact, the other defines the
response for the case in which no admissible source exists. The final block
constrains form rather than content, and belongs to the first function. Each of
these instructions is stated so that it can be checked against an output. That is
the property Chapter 4 requires of the requirement set $\Sigma$.

Whether an instruction of this kind is in fact obeyed is a separate question from
whether it has been written. Because the system prompt is expressed in natural
language rather than as a specification the generator is bound to satisfy, its
effect faces the same instruction-following and prompt-sensitivity issues as any
other instruction to a language model. Chapter 3 discusses those issues further.
A grounding constraint that is followed for one phrasing and disregarded for
another provides weaker protection than its wording suggests. The system prompt,
formalised in Chapter 4 as a set of requirements together with a wording, is
therefore the object of experimental control in this thesis.

\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation and Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:finetuning}

Fine-tuning is the other established way of adapting a general-purpose language
model to a specific application, and it is worth stating how it differs from
retrieval before Section~\ref{sec:usecase} turns to use cases. Fine-tuning
continues training on domain-specific data and changes the model parameters
$\theta$, so what the model learns enters the parametric memory described in
Section~\ref{sec:generator}. RAG leaves $\theta$ untouched and changes what is
placed in the context window at inference time. The two therefore act on
different parts of the system rather than competing to answer one question. The
practical decision is which part a given requirement belongs to: the knowledge
the system reasons over, or the way the system reasons and writes
\citep{ovadia2024finetuning}.

For knowledge that is new, changing, or proprietary, retrieval is the better fit,
for three reasons. The first is cost. Fine-tuning requires a training run over the
model's parameters whenever the underlying information changes. Adding a document
to a knowledge base requires only that the new chunks be embedded and indexed,
which is a fraction of the computation and can be done continuously rather than
in scheduled retraining cycles \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag}. The second is
data freshness and revocability. A document can be added, corrected, or deleted,
and the change takes effect at the next query, while a fact absorbed into $\theta$
cannot be selectively removed without retraining.
\citet{wang2024knowledgemechanisms} survey the difficulty of locating and editing
individual facts inside model parameters. This matters wherever information is
corrected or retracted, or must be deleted on request. It is also one reason
retrieval is preferred in regulated settings, where sensitive data can remain in
a controlled external store instead of entering the parameters
\citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. The third is auditability. Because a RAG answer is
produced from a specific set of retrieved chunks, each claim in the output can in
principle be checked against the passage that supports it, and unsupported claims
can be identified at the level of the individual statement \citep{niu2024ragtruth}.
A fine-tuned model has no comparable trace: the information is distributed across
its parameters, and the model cannot indicate which training example a given
assertion came from. Retrieval does not eliminate unsupported claims, as
Section~\ref{sec:usecase} discusses, but it does make them detectable.

Fine-tuning remains the correct choice when the requirement concerns behaviour
rather than facts \citep{zhou2023lima}. Three cases are typical. The first is
specialised vocabulary: in a domain whose terminology, abbreviations, or notation
are poorly represented in the training corpus, a model may misread the query or
the retrieved evidence itself, and no amount of retrieval repairs a failure that
occurs during interpretation. The second is strict output structure. A system
that must emit a fixed JSON schema, a proprietary markup or a domain-specific
programming language applies that constraint to every request, and such a
constraint is more reliably and more cheaply placed in the parameters than
restated in each prompt. The third is register and tone, where a consistent style
has to hold across many interactions rather than be described anew each time. In
each case fine-tuning adjusts how the model responds, not what it knows.

Framed this way, the two techniques are complementary rather than exclusive, and
production systems often combine them: a model fine-tuned for the vocabulary and
output format of its domain, retrieving the facts it reasons over from an
external knowledge base. The separation matters for this thesis because behaviour
can be controlled through either channel. Fine-tuning writes the desired
behaviour into $\theta$; the system prompt requests it in natural language at
inference time. The second channel is far cheaper and can be changed at any
moment, which is why most systems rely on it. Its effect, however, is the hardest
to predict, since a natural-language instruction is not a specification with
guaranteed semantics. This thesis holds $\theta$ fixed and studies that second
channel.

\section{Use-Case of RAG}
\label{sec:usecase}

RAG systems are typically used where the needed knowledge is too large, too
specific, or changes too often to be captured reliably by a model's parametric
knowledge alone. Deployments span several domains: question-answering over
enterprise and internal document collections \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag},
customer support systems that must answer from current product or policy
documentation, coding assistants that retrieve from a specific codebase instead
of relying on general programming knowledge, legal question-answering over case
law and contract repositories \citep{pipitone2024legalbenchrag}, and clinical
decision support that grounds its answers in current medical literature and
institutional protocols \citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. In each of these cases, RAG
is usually preferred over two alternatives. The first is fine-tuning the
generator on the target document collection, discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:finetuning}. The collections above change continuously, and
retraining on each change is neither economical nor traceable. The second is
extending the model's context window to include the whole collection. That avoids
retraining, but it does not scale to large collections, and it does not by itself
ensure that the model attends to the passages relevant to the query.

Despite these advantages, RAG does not remove the failure modes of purely
parametric generation; it shifts them, and in some cases only partly fixes them.
\citet{chen2024rgb} show that current language models tolerate a moderate amount
of irrelevant retrieved content reasonably well. They still struggle to reject a
query when none of the retrieved evidence is enough to answer it, to combine
evidence spread across multiple retrieved chunks, and to stay robust when
retrieved evidence is outdated or contradictory. \citet{niu2024ragtruth} further
show that language models keep producing hallucinated content even when relevant
evidence has been retrieved and given to them in context. Grounding a model in
retrieved evidence therefore does not, by itself, guarantee that its output stays
faithful to that evidence. These failure modes shape the rest of the thesis. The
literature review in Chapter 3 and the experimental design in Chapters 4 and 5
build the query set around directly answerable and multi-hop cases and vary the
quality of the retrieved evidence separately, so that the system prompt's effect
on them can be measured.
\section{Advanced RAG Architectures}
\label{sec:advanced}

The architecture described so far retrieves once per query and generates once
from what was retrieved. Recent work relaxes both assumptions, and the resulting
variants can be grouped by which stage of the pipeline they modify. The summary
below is for completeness; none of these variants is used in this thesis, which
studies the standard architecture.

\begin{description}
    \item[Conversational RAG.] Retaining prior turns of a dialogue as state, so that a follow-up question can be resolved against what was already asked and answered. A query such as ``and the second one?'' is meaningless to a retriever on its own, so the conversation history has to supply what is missing: either it is used to rewrite the query into a standalone form, or the retriever is trained directly on multi-turn data. \citet{liu2024chatqa} show that the second approach reaches comparable accuracy at lower cost than rewriting.
    \item[Pre-retrieval optimisation.] Rewriting or expanding the query before it reaches the retriever. Hypothetical Document Embeddings (HyDE) generate a hypothetical answer to the query and embed that instead of the query itself, on the argument that an answer lies closer in embedding space to the passage containing it than a question does \citep{gao2023hyde}.
    \item[Routing and adaptive retrieval.] Deciding where and how much to retrieve. Where several collections or indexes are available, a routing step can select among them according to what the query is about, rather than searching one combined collection for everything. Adaptive-RAG goes further and varies the depth of retrieval with the difficulty of the query, answering simple questions directly and reserving multi-step retrieval for those that need it \citep{jeong2024adaptiverag}.
    \item[Self-correction and reflection.] Evaluating retrieved evidence before or during generation. Corrective RAG (CRAG) grades each retrieved document, decomposes the ones it keeps into smaller knowledge strips, discards the strips that are not relevant, and falls back to a wider search when retrieval fails \citep{yan2024crag}. Self-RAG instead trains the model to decide for itself when to retrieve and to critique its own draft output, so that retrieval and generation interleave rather than run once each \citep{asai2024selfrag}.
    \item[Agentic RAG.] Treating retrieval as one action among several available to an agent. A meta-agent delegates to document-specific or tool-specific agents and combines their results, which turns a fixed pipeline into a workflow whose steps are chosen at run time \citep{singh2025agentic}.
\end{description}

Other work extends the architecture in directions that are orthogonal to this
routing taxonomy: knowledge graphs structure the retrieved evidence rather than
treating it as a flat set of passages \citep{edge2024graphrag}, and multimodal
retrieval covers images, audio and video alongside text
\citep{abootorabi2025multimodalrag}. A recent systematic review places these
directions within a single trajectory away from single-shot, text-only retrieval
\citep{oche2025systematicreview}. Understanding the standard architecture
formalised in this thesis remains a prerequisite for all of them. Each variant
changes the retrieval function $R$, the generation function $G$, or the number of
times they are applied, but none changes the underlying distribution
$p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$, which every variant still depends on.

This thesis implements the standard architecture and none of the variants above.
The reason is methodological. The research objective is to estimate the causal
effect of the system prompt on the output, which Chapter 4 formalises as the
intervention $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$. That effect is identifiable only as long as
the system prompt reaches the answer through a single path. Each of the variants
breaks that condition. Under Self-RAG the model decides for itself when to
retrieve \citep{asai2024selfrag}, so the system prompt influences which documents
are fetched as well as how they are used. Under routing, corrective and agentic
designs the prompt shapes the decisions of the router, the retrieval evaluator or
the meta-agent, so the retrieved context $C$ ceases to be determined by the query
and the corpus alone. Retrieval would then have to be written
$p_\eta(z \mid q,D,S)$ rather than $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$, and the arrow from $S$
into the retriever that Figure~\ref{fig:causal} deliberately omits would be
present. A measured difference between two prompt conditions could be attributed
either to the prompt's effect on generation or to its effect on what was
retrieved, with no way to separate the two. The routing policy would operate as a
confounding variable, and the isolation of effects that the experimental design
rests on would no longer hold.

The standard architecture is therefore the controlled baseline rather than a
simplification adopted for convenience. It is the configuration in which the
system prompt enters the pipeline at exactly one point, which is what makes the
intervention identifiable. The direction of inference matters as well. The
variants above add routing, reflection or delegation around the retrieval and
generation functions instead of replacing them, so an effect established here
bounds what can be expected once those mechanisms are introduced. An effect
measured inside an agentic pipeline, by contrast, could not be decomposed back
into a prompt component and a routing component. It is also the configuration
most widely deployed in practice, as Section~\ref{sec:usecase} describes, so the
result is not restricted to a hypothetical case. Establishing the causal mechanics
of prompt interventions at this level is a prerequisite for studying them in any
richer pipeline, and extending the design to one of the variants above is left to
future work.

\chapter{Literature Review}

Chapter 2 introduced the components of a RAG system. This chapter reviews three
areas of literature relevant to studying how the system prompt affects such a
system: Section 3.1 covers prompt sensitivity and instruction-following in
language models generally; Section 3.2 turns to retrieval-augmented generation,
its architecture, evaluation, and known failure modes; and Section 3.3 examines
adversarial and competing instructions delivered through retrieved content.
Section 3.4 brings the three together into the research gap this thesis addresses.

\section{Prompt Sensitivity and Instruction-Following in Language Models}
\label{sec:promptlit}

The first branch of literature studies language models with no retrieval
component at all. It divides on a distinction that determines what can be
inferred from it. One group manipulates the system prompt in the sense used here,
a standing instruction supplied in the system role and held apart from the user's
question. A larger group manipulates what this thesis calls the query, or the
task instruction and worked examples that accompany it in the user turn. That
group calls the manipulated object the prompt. Both groups report that behaviour
moves under changes that leave the propositional content of the instruction
intact, but only the first speaks directly to the variable manipulated here.

Three studies vary the system prompt itself. \citet{cheng2026systemprompts} vary
the system prompt given to instruction-tuned models for code generation, crossing
prompt specificity and prompting strategy with model, programming language and
decoding settings. They report that the system prompt moves the quality of
generated code independently of the task description. Theirs is the closest
published design to the one used here, and the present experiment carries it from
code generation into a retrieval-augmented setting, where the model is also given
evidence to work from. \citet{mu2025robustness} examine how reliably models honour
a system prompt when the user turn contains instructions conflicting with it.
Their result establishes the system prompt as a control whose authority is
contingent rather than guaranteed. \citet{zheng2024helpful} assign personas
through the system prompt across a large set of roles and find that doing so does
not reliably improve accuracy over one carrying no persona at all. That result is
worth stating separately, because it distinguishes changing the system prompt from
improving the system: an intervention on $S$ can alter behaviour without moving
any outcome measure in the intended direction.

A larger group varies the user-side prompt. \citet{lu2024sensitivity} compare
prompting techniques for in-context learning and measure sensitivity through
gradient-based saliency scores. They report that sensitivity correlates
negatively with accuracy and can serve as an unsupervised proxy for it.
\citet{zhuo2024prosa} propose ProSA, which scores instance-level variation in the
task instruction against decoding confidence, and report that larger models are
more robust to such variation and that few-shot examples reduce it.
\citet{salinas2024butterfly} perturb task prompts across text classification
tasks (adding a trailing space, requesting the output in XML, or prefixing a
jailbreak) and find that perturbations of this size change the labels the model
assigns. \citet{sclar2024quantifying} vary only the formatting of few-shot task
templates (separators, casing and spacing, none of which carries propositional
content) and report differences of up to 76 accuracy points between formats a
reader would judge equivalent. \citet{hua2025flaw} run twelve task templates
across several benchmarks and argue that much of the measured sensitivity is
produced by the evaluation method (log-likelihood scoring and rigid answer
matching) rather than by the model, since the variance falls once an LLM judge is
used instead. \citet{qin2026interactions} decompose a model's output score into
interactions between input variables and observe that small changes to the prompt
destabilise those interactions even where the final output is unchanged.

Two further studies sit outside both groups. \citet{ruiz2026promptcarefully} state
a rule in the instruction given to a model and then shift the contingencies that
rule was written for, and find that models keep following the stated rule after it
has stopped paying, a pattern that resembles rule-governed behaviour in humans.
The channel carrying the rule is not the variable of interest there, but the
finding bears on how a stated requirement competes with what the evidence in front
of the model supports. \citet{ying2024intuitive} study conflict between a model's
parametric memory and the evidence supplied in its context, and classify models as
intuitive or dependent by which source they favour. That conflict runs between
memory and context, not between a system prompt and a user instruction, so the
study bears on the evidence axis of this thesis rather than on its prompt axis.

Two conclusions follow, and they carry unequal weight. The first is that the
system prompt is a behavioural control variable whose effect can be isolated and
measured given a properly controlled setup, which the three system-prompt studies
support directly \citep{cheng2026systemprompts,mu2025robustness,zheng2024helpful}.
The second is that instructions are sensitive to changes that preserve their
meaning, whether lexical
\citep{lu2024sensitivity,zhuo2024prosa,salinas2024butterfly}, purely
formatting-level \citep{sclar2024quantifying}, or not visible in the output at all
\citep{qin2026interactions}, and that the apparent size of such an effect depends
on how it is measured \citep{hua2025flaw}. This second conclusion is established
for the user-side prompt, and its transfer to the system prompt is an assumption
rather than a result. The assumption is reasonable, since both texts reach the
same decoder as tokens in the same context window, but it is untested.
Instruction tuning also gives a reason it might fail: models are trained to treat
the system role as carrying more authority than the user role, which could make
$S$ either more stable under rewording or less. Which of these holds is part of
what this thesis sets out to determine rather than a premise it inherits. None of
these studies, in either group, works inside a retrieval-augmented setting, where
behaviour also depends on evidence that may be noisy, conflicting, or absent.

\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation: Architecture, Evaluation, and Failure Modes}

The second branch narrows to retrieval-augmented generation itself, and to the
failure modes it shows even when no one is manipulating the system prompt.

\citet{lewis2020rag} introduce Retrieval-Augmented Generation, combining a
pretrained neural retriever with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, and
show that conditioning generation on retrieved evidence outperforms purely
parametric models on knowledge-intensive tasks. Their probabilistic formulation,
in which retrieval is a distribution over documents and the retrieved document is
marginalised out, is the direct basis for the model formalised in Chapter 4.
\citet{gao2023survey} survey the development of RAG systems through Naive,
Advanced and Modular paradigms, organising the field around a tripartite
retrieval-generation-augmentation structure. Their taxonomy supports treating
retrieval and generation as separable functions whose interaction with the system
prompt can be studied independently, as this thesis does. Two later developments
extend this taxonomy in directions this thesis deliberately does not follow.
\citet{edge2024graphrag} restructure the retrieval side into a knowledge graph,
grouped into thematic communities and pre-summarised by an LLM, so that a query is
answered by traversing entities and community summaries rather than by a flat
nearest-neighbour lookup over $D$. \citet{singh2025agentic} and
\citet{jin2025searchr1} restructure the generation side into an agent loop, in
which the model itself decides when and what to retrieve, calling $R$ repeatedly
and adaptively rather than once per query; \citet{jin2025searchr1} train this
behaviour directly with reinforcement learning and report large gains over
standard RAG baselines. Both directions relax exactly the components Chapter 4
holds fixed: $R$ in the first case, and the single-call structure of $G$ in the
second. For that reason this thesis retains \citeauthor{gao2023survey}'s simplest,
Naive-RAG form: any change in the outcomes measured here can then be attributed to
the system prompt, rather than to the added architectural complexity of a
graph-structured retriever or an agentic generation loop.

\citet{es2023ragas} propose Ragas, a reference-free evaluation framework for RAG
pipelines built around three metrics that need no ground-truth human annotation:
faithfulness, answer relevance and context relevance. This thesis adopts two of
them directly as measures, faithfulness and answer relevance. The third, context
relevance, is a retrieval-side measure and is excluded for the reasons given in
Section~\ref{sec:metrics}. Their framework also motivates evaluating the effect of
the system prompt independently of retrieval quality. \citet{chen2024rgb}
construct the Retrieval-Augmented Generation Benchmark (RGB) and show that current
language models tolerate a moderate amount of retrieval noise but struggle with
negative rejection, with information integration across multiple retrieved
passages, and with robustness to counterfactual or contradictory evidence. Their
four testbeds map onto the experimental design of this thesis: the first two as
query categories, the remaining two as levels of retrieved-evidence quality.
\citet{niu2024ragtruth} introduce RAGTruth, a manually annotated corpus of
word-level hallucinations produced by RAG systems, and benchmark
hallucination-detection methods across multiple language models. Their finding
that hallucination persists even when relevant evidence has been retrieved
motivates measuring faithfulness as an outcome distinct from retrieval quality, as
this thesis does. \citet{yu2024ragevalsurvey} draw this body of work together into
a survey of RAG evaluation, separating the targets that assess the retrieval stage
from those that assess generation and naming three that belong to the latter:
relevance, faithfulness and correctness. The measures adopted in
Section~\ref{sec:metrics} come from that account, and this thesis's restriction to
generation-stage measurement rests on the separation it draws.

Together, these studies establish the architecture, evaluation vocabulary, and
known failure modes of RAG systems specifically. They show that a RAG system's
output quality depends on both retrieval quality and how faithfully the generator
uses the retrieved evidence \citep{es2023ragas,niu2024ragtruth}. Current systems
already struggle with certain conditions, such as conflicting or insufficient
retrieved evidence, even without any prompt manipulation \citep{chen2024rgb}.
None of the studies in this branch, however, treats the system prompt as a
controlled experimental variable; it is either fixed across conditions or not
specified at all.

\section{Adversarial and Competing Instructions in Retrieved Content}

A third line of work concerns instructions that arrive in the retrieved content rather than in the system prompt, and that compete with the system prompt for control of the model's behaviour. The first branch shows that a model's own system prompt can lose authority to instructions arriving in the user turn \citep{mu2025robustness}; \citet{greshake2023notwhat} go further, showing that text hidden inside a document the model is told to process can override the system prompt altogether. This is the same instruction competition taken to its adversarial extreme, except that the competing instruction reaches the model through the retrieved context $C$ rather than through $S$, which this thesis holds fixed rather than varies. \citet{zhang2025poisoning} build a RAG-specific benchmark on this basis, covering 13 poisoning attack methods and 7 defenses across 5 base question-answering datasets and 10 expanded variants. \citet{chen2024rgb} and \citet{niu2024ragtruth} in the second branch already find that retrieved evidence is often noisy, incomplete, or contradictory; the poisoning work adds that it can also be corrupted on purpose.

\medskip
Both studies, however, treat instruction competition exclusively as a security problem: an adversary deliberately crafts malicious content, and the goal is to detect or defend against it. Neither examines benign linguistic variation in the system prompt itself, the kind the prompt-sensitivity literature reviewed above studies outside of RAG, and the kind this thesis's conditions ($S{b,0}$ through $S{b,4}$, for each baseline $b$) are built to isolate inside it. The question here is therefore not whether a RAG system can be attacked, but whether ordinary, well-intentioned rewordings of its own instructions change its behaviour at all.

\section{Synthesis and Research Gap}

The first branch shows two things separately: that the system prompt is a controllable way of shaping language model behaviour, and that instructions are sensitive to rewording that leaves their meaning intact. It establishes the controllability result for the system prompt, the sensitivity result mostly for the user-side prompt, and both outside any retrieval-augmented setting. The second branch establishes the architecture, evaluation vocabulary, and known failure modes of RAG systems, including problems with evidence integration, rejection, and faithfulness, but does not treat the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable. The third branch shows that instructions can compete with the system prompt in RAG systems and that this competition is actively studied, but only as a deliberate security threat delivered through retrieved content, never as benign, controlled linguistic variation of the system prompt itself. The overlap between all three branches is still largely unexamined. \citet{ying2024intuitive} comes nearest, but the conflict studied there runs between a model's parametric memory and its retrieved context rather than between two wordings of one instruction, and neither retrieval nor the underlying model is held fixed as an explicit experimental control.

This thesis addresses that gap. It treats the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable inside a RAG pipeline, holding the query, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and underlying language model fixed, so that any change in the system's behaviour and outcomes can be traced to the prompt itself. Four of the five conditions vary only the wording of the instructions and leave what they require untouched, which separates the effect of phrasing from the effect of content. The fifth alters the requirements themselves by weakening their modal force, and the analysis keeps it apart from the other four so that the two kinds of change are not conflated.

The experimental design of this thesis is grounded in the causal inference framework for natural language processing set out by \citet{feder2022causalnlp}, who consolidate work scattered across several fields into a single account of what it means to estimate a causal effect when the variables of interest are texts. Their organising distinction is between the three roles a text can occupy in a causal question: it may be the outcome, the confounder, or the treatment. Here the text is the treatment. The system prompt is assigned by the experimenter rather than observed in the wild, and the quantity of interest is the effect of assigning one such text rather than another. Two of their arguments bear directly on the design that follows. The first is that a causal question about language demands that the intervention be formalised before it can be estimated, since natural language admits no self-evident notion of what it would mean to change one thing and hold the rest constant. The second is that where domain knowledge does not supply a small set of admissible texts, controllable generation offers a way to assign texts that differ in only one latent aspect, changing the style of a text while holding its content fixed. The prompt conditions used here are built on that second argument: the set of requirements a prompt states is held fixed while the wording expressing them is varied, so that the assigned texts differ along a single axis. Applying the framework to a retrieval-augmented system adds a demand their single-model examples do not face: the treatment must be shown to reach the outcome along one path only, which is why the retriever and the evidence it returns are held fixed throughout. Chapter 4 builds the formal model that makes this control precise. It describes the RAG system as a retrieval function combined with a generation function, with the system prompt as an explicit parameter of the generation function, and states the assignment of a prompt condition as an intervention on that model.

\chapter{Formal Model of a RAG System}

Chapter 3 identified a gap: prompt sensitivity is studied outside retrieval, and RAG evaluation does not treat the system prompt as a controlled variable. Closing it requires a model of the system precise enough to isolate the system prompt. This chapter builds that model on the probabilistic formulation of retrieval-augmented generation given by \citet{lewis2020rag}, and extends it by making the system prompt an explicit variable of the generation process.

\section{Modelling Objective}

The formalisation does two things. It states the probabilistic relationship between the user query, the retrieved documents and the generated output. It also provides the basis for treating the system prompt as an experimental intervention whose effect can be measured while the query, the knowledge base, the retriever and the language model are held constant.

The model can be read as a chain,
[
(q,D) ;\longrightarrow; p\eta(z \mid q,D) ;\longrightarrow; p\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y{1:i-1},S) ;\longrightarrow; p{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S),
]
in which the system prompt $S$ enters at the generation stage only. The research question of this thesis concerns that final distribution: what changes in $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ when $S$ is changed?

The symbols used below are those listed in the Notation section of Chapter 1. The one departure from \citet{lewis2020rag} is that they write the input sequence as $x$, whereas this thesis writes it as $q$, since the input is always a user query; their notation is otherwise kept unchanged.

\section{Probabilistic Retrieval}

Retrieval is represented as a probability distribution over documents rather than as a single choice:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:retrieval}
p_\eta(z \mid q,D).
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval} reads as the probability the retriever assigns to document $z$, given the query $q$ and the knowledge base $D$. Writing retrieval this way means the retriever never has to commit to one document being the correct one. It spreads probability over the candidates, and documents it considers more relevant receive more of it.

A knowledge base may hold far more documents than can be evaluated in practice. The formulation therefore keeps only the $K$ documents with the highest retrieval probability,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:topk}
\mathcal{Z}K(q,D) = \operatorname*{TopK}{z \in D} ; p_\eta(z \mid q,D),
\end{equation}

and the retrieved context handed to the generator is the resulting collection,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:context}
C = {z_1,\ldots,z_K}, \qquad z_k \in \mathcal{Z}_K(q,D).
\end{equation}

The distinction between $z$ and $C$ matters later: the first is a candidate the model sums over, the second the concrete collection the running system retrieves and logs.

\section{Prompt-Conditioned Generation}

\citet{lewis2020rag} write the generator at the level of a single token as $p\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y{1:i-1})$: the probability of the next token given the query, one retrieved document, and the tokens already produced. This thesis adds the system prompt as a further conditioning variable:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:token}
p\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:token} is the point at which this thesis departs from the original formulation, and the departure is deliberately small. The generator is the same model with the same parameters $\theta$; what changes is that the system prompt is now written into the conditioning information rather than left implicit.

Because generation is autoregressive, the probability of a complete output sequence follows from the token probabilities by multiplication:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sequence}
p\theta(y \mid q,z,S) = \prod{i=1}^{L} p\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

\section{Marginalizing over Retrieved Documents}
\label{sec:ragdist}

Equation~\eqref{eq:sequence} gives the probability of an output under one particular document $z$. Since the system does not know which retrieved document supports the answer, the model averages over the candidates, weighting each by its retrieval probability. \citet{lewis2020rag} give two ways of doing this, which differ only in where the sum is placed.

\subsection{RAG-Sequence}

RAG-Sequence assumes that a single retrieved document is responsible for the whole answer. The probability of an output is the weighted average, over documents, of the probability that document would produce that entire sequence:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragseq}
p{\text{RAG-Sequence}}(y \mid q,D,S) ;\approx; \sum{z \in \mathcal{Z}K(q,D)} p\eta(z \mid q,D) \prod{i=1}^{L} p\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

The sum stands outside the product, and that is what encodes the assumption: one document is fixed, the entire sequence is generated from it, and only then is the next document considered.

\subsection{RAG-Token}

RAG-Token makes the weaker assumption that different parts of an answer may come from different documents. The average over documents is taken separately at every token:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragtok}
p{\text{RAG-Token}}(y \mid q,D,S) ;\approx; \prod{i=1}^{L} \sum{z \in \mathcal{Z}K(q,D)} p\eta(z \mid q,D) , p\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

The whole difference between the two is the order of the two operators: $\sum_z \prod_i$ against $\prod_i \sum_z$. RAG-Sequence commits to one document for the length of the answer; RAG-Token lets one document supply the first half of a sentence and another the second, the more flexible assumption and the one better suited to answers that combine several sources.

Where the distinction does not matter, both are written here as $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$. It does not matter for the experiment: the system prompt $S$ appears in exactly the same place in both, inside the generator and nowhere else.

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragdist}
p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S).
\end{equation}

\section{The System Prompt: Requirements and Wording}
\label{sec:promptreq}

The system prompt enters Equation~\eqref{eq:token} as a single symbol, but it is a text with internal structure, and the experiment varies that structure deliberately. A prompt states a set of requirements and states them in particular words. Writing the two separately gives

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prompt}
S = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi), \qquad \Sigma = {\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m},
\end{equation}

which separates what a prompt asks for from the words it asks in. Two prompts built from the same $\Sigma$ ask for the same thing; two built from the same $\varphi$ say things the same way.

The conditions cross two factors. The first is the requirement set. Three are used, each taken from a prompt published with a retrieval-augmented system and reproduced in Section~\ref{sec:procedure}. Writing $\Sigma_A$, $\Sigma_B$ and $\Sigma_C$ for the three: $\Sigma_A$ is the response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}, which states a single requirement; $\Sigma_B$ is the VANILLA instruction of \citet{gao2023alce}, which states about ten clauses falling into four independently checkable families; and $\Sigma_C$ is the instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}, which its authors describe as three parts. The sets differ in $m$ by an order of magnitude between $\Sigma_A$ and $\Sigma_B$, with $\Sigma_C$ lying between them.

The second factor is the wording. Four variants are written for each requirement set, holding $\Sigma$ fixed and changing only $\varphi$, and a fifth changes the requirement set itself. The axes along which the wording is varied are taken from three prior studies rather than devised here. \citet{wahle2024paraphrase} vary prompts by linguistically defined paraphrase type rather than by ad hoc rewriting, and group those types into families, of which morphology, syntax and lexicon apply to a standing instruction. \citet{zhan2024lexical} isolate the lexical axis by substituting single semantically similar words, and report that models are over-sensitive to variations imperceptible to a reader. \citet{kostic2026samemeaning} separate a lexical pipeline built on synonym substitution from a syntactic one built on dependency parsing, and find that the two behave differently: lexical perturbation degrades performance consistently, syntactic perturbation does not. This thesis follows their approach of varying a prompt along linguistically defined, meaning-preserving axes one at a time, and applies it to the standing system prompt of a retrieval-augmented system. Its research question requires that application, and none of the three studies addresses it. Writing $S_{b,j}$ for the $j$-th wording of baseline $b$:
\begin{description}
    \item[$S_{b,0} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_0)$, canonical.] The published form, unchanged. The reference the other four are measured against.
    \item[$S_{b,1} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_1)$, lexical.] Synonym substitution with clause order and voice held fixed.
    \item[$S_{b,2} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_2)$, syntactic.] Clause reordering and active to passive, with the vocabulary held as far as English allows.
    \item[$S_{b,3} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_3)$, format.] The same wording laid out as a list rather than as prose.
    \item[$S_{b,4} = \rho(\Sigma_b',\varphi_0)$, deontic.] Canonical wording with every obligation weakened to a request.
\end{description}

Fifteen conditions follow. Each axis changes one thing and holds the rest fixed. That is what makes a difference between two conditions attributable to that axis, and it is easy to break by accident. The syntactic variant carries obligation uniformly through the construction \textit{is to be}, so that a change of voice does not also change modal force; the format variant writes its items as bare noun phrases for the same reason. Modal force belongs to the deontic condition alone.

Baseline $A$ states the fewest requirements and is short enough to give in full. The five conditions read:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The five conditions of baseline $A$, whose canonical form is the prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}.},label={lst:baselineA}]
A0  Use the following pieces of context to answer the question.

A1  Employ the following excerpts of material to address the query.

A2  The question is to be answered using the context pieces below.

A3  CONTEXT: the following pieces
    TASK: use them to answer the question

A4  Please try to answer the question using the context pieces below.
\end{lstlisting}

The variants for baselines $B$ and $C$ are written on the same principle and are longer in proportion to their requirement sets. Within each baseline the five conditions are held to within roughly a tenth of the canonical form in length, so that a difference between two of them cannot be read as an effect of how much text the prompt contains rather than of the axis being varied. The deontic condition resists this: weakening an obligation to a request adds words, and forcing it back to the canonical length would mean cutting a requirement, which would change $\Sigma$ rather than $\varphi$. That condition is therefore allowed to run slightly long, and the word counts of all fifteen conditions are recorded so that length can be entered as a covariate if the results call for it.

Crossing the wording axes with three requirement sets rather than one is what lets the central question be asked properly. If the system responded only to what a prompt requires and not to how it is written, $S{b,0}$ through $S{b,3}$ would produce the same output distribution within every baseline, since they differ in $\varphi$ alone. Whether they do is the empirical question. The format condition in particular follows \citet{sclar2024quantifying}, who report large performance differences from formatting changes of this kind. They apply those changes to few-shot task templates rather than to a system prompt, so carrying the manipulation across to $S$ is itself part of what is tested here. Using three baselines also answers an objection a single one cannot. A prompt stating one requirement leaves the deontic condition almost nothing to weaken, while a prompt stating ten risks confounding the wording manipulation with the length of the text. Observing the same wording effect across requirement sets that differ by an order of magnitude in $m$ separates the effect of wording from the effect of density; observing it in only one leaves the two joined.

Every contrast is taken within a baseline. Comparing $S{B,1}$ against $S{A,0}$ would confound a change of wording with a change in what is asked for, so each variant is measured against the canonical form of its own requirement set.

\section{The System Prompt as an Intervention}
\label{sec:intervention}

The prompt appears in the generator (Equation~\eqref{eq:token}) and not in the retriever (Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval}). The retrieval term is therefore written without $S$ among its arguments: the system prompt is supplied to the generator but plays no part in choosing documents. In the notation of \citet{pearl2009causality}, $S$ has an effect on $y$ and no effect on $z$. Figure~\ref{fig:causal} shows the resulting structure.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style   = {draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=9mm, minimum width=21mm,
                    align=center, font=\small},
    tx/.style    = {draw, very thick, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=9mm,
                    minimum width=21mm, align=center, font=\small},
    ar/.style    = {-{Latex[length=2mm]}},
    lb/.style    = {font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt, fill=white, align=center}
]
\node[box] (q)    at (0,1.4)    {$q$};
\node[box] (d)    at (0,-1.4)   {$D$};
\node[box] (retr) at (3.9,-1.4) {Retriever $\eta$};
\node[box] (z)    at (7.6,-1.4) {$z$};
\node[tx]  (s)    at (7.6,2.4)  {$S$};
\node[box] (gen)  at (11.3,0)   {Generator $\theta$};
\node[tx]  (y)    at (14.9,0)   {$y$};
\node[box] (m)    at (18.4,0)   {$M(Y)$};

\draw[ar] (q) -- (retr);
\draw[ar] (d) -- (retr);
\draw[ar] (retr) -- (z);
\draw[ar] (z) -- (gen);
\draw[ar] (q) -- (gen);
\draw[ar, very thick] (s) -- (gen);
\draw[ar] (gen) -- (y);
\draw[ar] (y) -- (m);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
\caption{The system prompt $S$ and the output $y$ are drawn in bold. $S$ enters the generator and has no arrow into the retriever, which is what allows a change in $y$ to be attributed to a change in $S$.}
\label{fig:causal}
\end{figure}

For the effect of the prompt to be interpretable, the following are held constant across conditions. Each query is used unchanged in every condition it is run in, so that every comparison is made within a query rather than between queries, and the knowledge base is fixed at $D = D_0$. The retriever and its parameters are fixed at $\eta = \eta_0$, and the language model at $\theta = \theta_0$, so the model is neither retrained nor replaced between conditions. Retrieval is assumed prompt-independent,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:noedge}
p_\eta(z \mid q,D,S) = p_\eta(z \mid q,D),
\end{equation}

which holds by construction for the single-shot architecture of Chapter 2 and is enforced in the experiment by recording the retrieved context once and reusing it. Generation, by contrast, is prompt-dependent: although $\theta$ stays fixed, the conditional distribution it defines may differ between $S_0$ and $S_j$. Finally, decoding is stochastic, so the same configuration can produce different outputs from one run to the next; the term $\varepsilon$ carries that variation:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:noise}
Y = G(q,z,S;\theta,\varepsilon),
\end{equation}

which is why each condition is repeated several times in Chapter 5.

\section{The Causal Effect}

Setting the system prompt to a fixed value is an intervention, written $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ following \citet{pearl2009causality}. The notation separates \textit{setting} a variable from merely \textit{observing} it: observing that two systems with different prompts give different answers leaves open the possibility that something else differed too, whereas setting the prompt while holding the rest fixed does not. For a query $q$ under prompt condition $S_j$, the output of the system is the random variable
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:potential}
Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S_j).
\end{equation}

For one query the effect of the prompt is the difference in the expected value of the measured property $M(Y)$ between the two conditions. That difference is not, however, the quantity of interest. A prompt that improves the answer to one question and degrades the answer to the next has established no effect at all, and a difference observed on a single input cannot be separated from the particular difficulty, phrasing or subject matter of that input. The research question asks about the behaviour of the system over the queries it will actually meet, so the effect has to be defined as an average over the distribution from which queries are drawn. Writing $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ for that distribution over the query space $\mathcal{Q}$, the causal effect of prompt condition $S_j$ relative to the baseline $S_0$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:estimand}
\tau_j \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{q \sim P_{\mathcal{Q}}}\Bigl[\; \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_j)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr] \;-\; \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_0)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr] \;\Bigr],
\end{equation}

which connects the probability model of Section~\ref{sec:ragdist} directly to the quantity the experiment estimates. Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is what Chapter 5 measures.

Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is an average treatment effect in the standard sense: the query plays the role of the unit, the prompt condition the role of the treatment, and $\tau_j$ the mean over units of the difference between the two treatments. Two features of how it is written matter. The difference is taken inside the outer expectation rather than between two separately averaged quantities. That is what makes each query its own control, and it matches the paired design of Section~\ref{sec:intervention}. By linearity of expectation the two forms agree, but only the form given here records the pairing. The expectations are also nested rather than pooled: the inner expectation averages over the randomness of retrieval and decoding at a fixed query, while the outer averages over the queries themselves, so a large effect confined to a handful of inputs is not mistaken for a general one.

The distribution $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ is not known and cannot be written down. Chapter 5 replaces it with the empirical distribution of a finite query set. That is the point at which the choice of data becomes a claim about generality rather than a matter of convenience, and it is why Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase} draws the queries from established question-answer datasets rather than composing them for the experiment.

Two points about the interpretation. The intervention is on the conditioning variable $S$, not on the model parameters: $\theta$ remains $\theta_0$ throughout, and the prompt changes what the generator is conditioned on rather than what it has learned. Because $S$ is assigned by the experimenter rather than observed, no further assumption about hidden common causes is needed; all that remains to be established is that the conditions in Section~\ref{sec:intervention} actually hold in the implementation.

The choice of $M$ is left open here. Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} describes what that property has to capture, and Section~\ref{sec:metrics} fixes it.

\section{Grounding the Knowledge Base in Established Datasets}
\label{sec:knowledgebase}

The knowledge base has so far been written as a set $D = {d_1,\ldots,d_N}$ of unanalysed objects, and the model says nothing about where those objects come from. Filling $D$ is a methodological decision rather than a further step of formalisation. It is settled here because the effect defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is only as informative as the corpus and the queries it is estimated over.

Two routes are available. The first is to define the corpus theoretically: fix a set of facts about a small invented subject area, generate documents from those facts, and derive the query set from the resulting support structure. Its advantage is that every fact is known to the experimenter by construction. One defect is that the data model and the hypothesis are then written by the same hand, so the difficulty of the queries, the density of distractors and the frequency of contradictions all become free parameters chosen in full knowledge of the effect they are meant to reveal. A result obtained on such a corpus is also incomparable with anything else in the field, because no other system has ever been run on it.

The second route, taken here, is to instantiate $D$ and the query set from established question-answer datasets. Three properties recommend it. The questions are naturally occurring rather than authored for the experiment, so the query distribution is not fitted to the hypothesis it is used to test. The corpora are public, versioned and split in a documented way, so a third party can repeat the measurement without reconstructing a private data generator. And because published results for a wide range of retrieval and generation systems already exist on these datasets, the effect estimated in Chapter 5 can be read against a background of prior work instead of standing alone. Reproducibility and comparability are properties of the benchmark, not of the formalism, and no amount of additional notation supplies them.

Only MS MARCO is used. Its queries were issued to Bing by people looking for something rather than composed for a benchmark, and they are not confined to a subject area, so the collection spans a wide and uneven range of topics. That heterogeneity is the reason for the choice. An instruction that governs behaviour on one domain and fails on another has established little about the system prompt, and a corpus narrow enough to have been memorised would let a model score well without consulting the retrieved evidence. Drawing the whole query set from one heterogeneous collection also holds the annotation convention fixed, which a mixture of sources with different labelling rules would not.

The collection is built from queries sampled from Bing search logs, with candidate passages retrieved for each query and free-form answers written by human judges \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}. Its passage-ranking split supplies the relevance judgements on which dense retrievers are commonly trained and evaluated, and that is the role it plays here. The dense retrieval architecture itself follows \citet{karpukhin2020dpr}, whose dual encoder was shown to retrieve more accurately than a sparse lexical baseline on Natural Questions and four further open-domain benchmarks. For the present purpose MS MARCO governs the retrieval half of the pipeline: it supplies query-passage pairs whose relevance was judged externally rather than asserted by the experimenter. That is what a study of the system prompt requires, since the prompt must be shown to act on the generator and not on the evidence reaching it. Its annotations also mark the items for which no supplied passage answers the query, and the abstention category of Section~\ref{sec:corpus} is drawn from that material.

MS MARCO provides the queries $q$, the documents that populate $D$, and, through its annotations, both the reference answer $a_q$ and the fact set $\Phi$ against which an output can be judged. The point of the empirical route is that $\Phi$ is fixed by the annotation and by the indexed passages, not by the experimenter.

What it does not provide is a definition of a good answer. An annotation records which answer is correct; it says nothing about whether an answer is grounded in the evidence actually retrieved, whether it addresses the question directly rather than evading it, or whether it obeys the requirements the system prompt states. Those properties are the object of this thesis, and defining them is the subject of the next section.
\section{What Counts as a Good Answer}
\label{sec:goodanswer}

Nothing established so far says what would count as an improvement. The preceding sections describe what the system does and how the influence of the system prompt can be separated from everything else that shapes an output, but any comparison between prompt conditions presupposes a standard against which outputs are judged, and no such standard follows from the architecture itself. It has to be stated, and stating it belongs to the model rather than to the implementation: the trustworthiness of a measurement depends on the notion of quality it estimates, and that notion has to be fixed before any output is inspected.

Quality in a retrieval-augmented system is not one property but several, and they can fail independently of one another. The first is groundedness. An answer should rest on the documents the retriever actually supplied, and an answer that introduces material from nowhere has failed even if the material happens to be true. The second is relevance. An answer should address the question that was asked and address it directly, so that hedging, padding and drift towards a neighbouring question all count as failures, as does supplying unrelated material where the evidence will not support a reply at all. The third is correctness. An answer should agree with what is in fact the case, which is a different demand from the first and is treated separately here for a reason given below. The fourth is obedience. Whatever the system prompt asks for should be visible in the output itself, whether in length, in form, or in the handling of missing evidence, since a prompt that is not followed cannot be said to have any effect on quality at all.

All four are properties of the text the generator produces, and that restriction is deliberate. The system prompt reaches the answer only through the generator, so a standard that also graded the ranking returned by the retriever would be grading something the prompt cannot touch. Each of the four is estimated by a measure taken from published work rather than invented here: groundedness by faithfulness, which asks whether every claim can be traced to a supplied passage; relevance by answer relevance, which asks whether the response addresses the question put to it; correctness by agreement with the reference answer recorded in the data; and obedience by whether the output satisfies every instruction the prompt states.

Groundedness and correctness are kept apart because collapsing them would hide the failure this thesis is concerned with. The retrieved context is neither complete nor guaranteed to be free of error. An answer can therefore be correct while ignoring the evidence entirely, drawing instead on what the generator already knows, and it can use the evidence faithfully and still be wrong because the evidence was wrong. A system that scores well on correctness alone reveals nothing about whether retrieval was used, which is precisely the behaviour a system prompt is supposed to govern.

Answers are rarely either flawless or worthless, so a standard that only sorts them into good and bad discards most of what an experiment can observe. A graded notion of shortfall is needed instead, one that records how far an answer falls from the standard and in which of the four respects it does so. The four are also not equally serious, and their relative weight is a property of the setting rather than of the model. A claim that no retrieved document supports is a grave failure in a clinical assistant and a minor irritation in a consumer search tool, and any honest account of quality has to leave room for that difference to be recorded rather than assumed away. Grading the shortfall in this way settles the question of abstention without a separate rule: where a wrong answer is treated as costlier than an admission of ignorance, declining to answer becomes the better option exactly when the evidence will not support a reply. That is the long-standing trade-off between committing errors and withholding judgement described by \citet{chow1970reject}.

The evaluation of this thesis therefore asks a single question. Holding the query, the knowledge base and the retrieval procedure fixed, does replacing one system prompt with another move the outputs of the system towards this standard or away from it, and does the direction of that movement survive when the retrieved evidence is degraded? A baseline prompt supplies the point of comparison, and every other condition is read against it. Because the four properties are separable, the answer can be reported both as a single summary of overall quality and as a breakdown showing which of the four the prompt improved and which, if any, it made worse.

The formal definitions this evaluation rests on are introduced in Chapter 5. That chapter states the set of acceptable answers precisely, gives the cost function that grades an answer's shortfall against the four properties, fixes faithfulness, answer relevance, correctness and instruction adherence as the measures that estimate them, and defines the quantity that expresses the effect of a prompt condition relative to the baseline. It also specifies the corpus, the query set, the experimental conditions and the statistical procedure through which that quantity is estimated.

\chapter{Experimental Setup}

This chapter turns the model of Chapter 4 into a concrete procedure. Chapter 4 described the system as the distribution $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:ragdist} and the effect of a prompt as the intervention $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ on it. The system under test is the standard architecture, for the reasons of experimental control given in Section~\ref{sec:advanced}. The chapter describes how the corpus and the query set are assembled from the datasets of Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase}, which conditions the system is run under, how the parts of the cost $\ell$ are measured, and how the effect $\tau_j$ is estimated. All experiments are implemented in Python. Every metric used below is taken from published RAG evaluation work, and every design parameter follows a precedent named in the text. Section~\ref{sec:metrics} names the measure used for each part of the standard that Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} set out in general terms, and Section~\ref{sec:procedure} states that standard formally before any estimator is defined, so that no metric is chosen before the notion of quality it estimates has been fixed.

\section{Goal and Design}
\label{sec:goal}

The experiment manipulates exactly one component of the pipeline. The system prompt is the sole independent variable; every other part of the system is held at a fixed value for the whole of the data collection. This is a deliberate control rather than a convenience. A retrieval-augmented system has many parts that are known to move answer quality on their own, and each of them, if left free, would offer an alternative explanation for any difference observed between prompt conditions. Fixing them removes those explanations in advance, so that a difference in the measured cost can be attributed to the prompt and to nothing else.

The components held constant are the following. The generator is a single instruction-tuned language model, fixed in both its identity and its parameters $\theta$ for the whole of the data collection, and its decoding temperature and maximum output length are fixed with it, since the temperature of Equation~\eqref{eq:temperature} governs how far the sampling distribution is flattened and would otherwise be confounded with the prompt. Queries and passages are encoded by one and the same embedding model, applied to both sides of the comparison in the manner of the dense retriever of \citet{karpukhin2020dpr}. The vector database and the index built over it, implemented with the similarity-search library of \citet{johnson2021faiss}, are constructed once before any condition is run and are never rebuilt thereafter. The chunking strategy $\chi$ is one passage per chunk. \citet{chen2024densex} compare retrieval units of different granularity and report that finer units than the passage retrieve better, so the passage is fixed here not because it is the strongest choice but because granularity must be held constant for the prompt contrast to be interpretable, and the passage is the unit in which the source datasets are already distributed. The remaining components are likewise fixed. Because the retriever and its parameters $\eta$ are determined entirely by these choices, they do not vary either. None of these is re-tuned, re-indexed or re-selected at any point during the experiment, and no condition is run against a rebuilt index.

Fixing the retrieval side has a second consequence that matters for the causal claim, and varying the depth does not weaken it. Because $\eta$, $\chi$, the embedding model and the index are all constant, the set $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:topk} depends only on the query and on the depth at which it is taken, and never on the system prompt. Within a depth level, therefore, the same passages are retrieved in every prompt condition, and every condition receives exactly the same evidence. Any change in the answer originates at the generator, which is exactly the localisation the intervention of Chapter 4 assumes. Because every prompt contrast is estimated within a depth level, the depth axis multiplies the number of cells in which that contrast is measured without entering any contrast itself.

Two further factors vary, and neither is a second treatment. The first is the quality of the evidence placed in the context, set to four levels and described in Section~\ref{sec:conditions}, because a prompt effect that appears only when the evidence is clean would be of little practical interest. The second is the retrieval depth, set to $K \in \{0,5,10\}$ passages per query. Depth is included for the same kind of reason as evidence quality rather than for one of its own: an instruction that governs behaviour when the context holds five passages has established little if it fails once the context holds ten, and the amount of material a prompt has to govern is exactly the sort of deployment parameter that differs between applications reusing the same pipeline. The three values are chosen to bracket that range rather than to sample it finely. $K = 5$ is the reference, the smallest depth at which the context can hold the gold evidence a multi-hop query needs together with a realistic number of distractors; $K = 10$ doubles the material without changing anything else, so that the prompt is asked to govern a longer and noisier context; and $K = 0$ supplies no retrieved passage at all and therefore coincides by construction with the empty evidence level $c^{\varnothing}$ of Section~\ref{sec:conditions}, which is already part of the design. The depth axis accordingly adds one new set of cells, those at $K = 10$, and folds the existing empty-context control into its lower end rather than introducing a third condition alongside it.

Both are controlled robustness factors in the same sense: the contrast of interest is always between prompt conditions, estimated separately within each level of each factor, and the levels themselves are not compared to make a claim about them. Depth is fixed before any prompt condition is applied and is never re-selected during a run, so within a level it is as constant as the components listed above; what changes is that the experiment is now run at more than one of its values. A single observation of the design is then

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:design}
y_{ijkr} \;\sim\; p_{\mathrm{RAG}}\bigl(y \;\big|\; q_i,\ \mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik}),\ \mathrm{do}(S = S_j)\bigr), \qquad r = 1,\ldots,n,
\end{equation}

The fifteen prompt conditions and the four evidence levels are fully crossed, giving $60$ cells at the reference depth. The depth axis applies only where the context is actually retrieved, that is at $c^{\text{ret}}$, since the other three levels set $C$ directly and do not consult the retriever at all; running that level at $K = 10$ as well as at $K = 5$ therefore adds a further fifteen cells, and $K = 0$ adds none, being the empty level already counted. The design comprises $75$ cells in all. Every query is run in every cell, so each query is its own control and no difference between conditions can be produced by a difference in the queries they were asked.

Within a cell the only remaining source of variation is the random term $\varepsilon$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:noise}, which is why each cell is repeated $n = 5$ times. Five is the repetition budget of \citet{zhuo2024prosa}, whose study of prompt sensitivity repeats generation under a fixed prompt so that the sensitivity being measured can be separated from the noise of sampling, and it is adopted here for three reasons rather than taken over on authority. The first concerns what a repetition buys. Each generation in a cell is a draw from the same distribution $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S_j)$, so averaging $n$ of them leaves the quantity being estimated unchanged and reduces the standard error of the cell mean by a factor of $\sqrt{n}$. Going from three draws to five is therefore not a change of estimand but a change of resolution: it narrows the interval around every $\hat{\tau}_{jk}$ without altering what $\hat{\tau}_{jk}$ means, which matters here because the differences this experiment looks for are differences between wordings of the same requirement set and are expected to be small. The second concerns who fixes the number. A repetition count settled after inspecting the variance of the first cells would let the sample size be tuned to the effect it is meant to detect, which is a degree of freedom the experimenter should not hold in a study whose entire claim rests on having removed alternative explanations in advance; taking the count from published practice settles it before any output is seen. The third is that the choice must remain affordable at the scale the full crossing imposes, and at sixty cells and one hundred queries five repetitions is the largest budget that keeps the run tractable. Where the three pull against one another it is the second that decides, since a defensible experiment is worth more here than a marginally tighter interval. \citet{salinas2024butterfly} remove this term instead, decoding at temperature $0$ so that one generation per condition suffices. That is not the setting studied here. A deployed system samples, and a prompt effect worth reporting is one that survives sampling, so the term is retained and averaged over rather than switched off. Seventy-five cells, one hundred queries and five repetitions give 37{,}500 generations.

\section{Knowledge Base and Query Set}
\label{sec:corpus}

The corpus is collected, not generated. Following Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase}, the knowledge base $D$ is assembled from the MS MARCO passage collection \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}. The index holds every passage attached to a validation row whose \texttt{wellFormedAnswers} field is non-empty, $N = 122{,}678$ passages in total. No distractor pool is sampled: the gold passages for a query compete against the whole corpus, and whatever else the retriever ranks above them is a distractor the collection itself supplied. Because 0.38\,GB of float32 vectors fits in memory at this size, the index is exhaustive rather than approximate, so retrieval depends on the query alone and not on how the index was built. Nothing in $D$ is written for this thesis, and the annotations, not the experimenter, fix which passage answers which query.

What the empirical route gives up is the closed world. On an invented corpus the fact set $\Phi$ is complete by construction, so any claim in an output can be checked against it. Here $\Phi$ contains the annotated gold answers and the facts stated by the passages in $D$, which is a subset of everything true. Correctness in the sense of Equation~\eqref{eq:ideal} is therefore evaluated only over the claims that bear on $a_q$: an output asserting some further true fact absent from $\Phi$ is not counted against requirement 3. Section~\ref{sec:limits} states what this costs. The gain is that the retrieval judgements, the answer annotations and the queries themselves come from outside the experiment, so the difficulty of the query set was not chosen by the person testing the hypothesis, and a third party can rebuild the index from the published datasets.

Each passage forms one chunk, for the reason given in Section~\ref{sec:goal}, and passages are used at the length at which they were published, which runs to between two and six sentences. Retrieval returns the $K$ chunks with the highest retrieval probability, the set $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:topk}, at each of the depths $K \in \{5,10\}$ at which the retrieved level is run. The composition of the top five is not engineered. A direct query needs one gold passage among the five and a multi-hop query needs two or more, and whether they arrive is a property of the retriever and the corpus rather than a quantity set in advance. This is stricter than the distractor setting of \citet{yang2018hotpotqa}, in which two gold paragraphs are placed among eight distractors by construction, and it is what the retrieval gate of Section~\ref{sec:procedure} exists to measure. The gate is applied separately at each depth, since a query whose gold evidence is missed at $K = 5$ may be recovered at $K = 10$. The position of the gold passages inside the context is randomised and recorded, since \citet{liu2024lostmiddle} report that accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits.

Because the retriever is fixed before any condition is run, how well it performs is a property of the experimental control and can be reported once, ahead of the results, and it is reported at each depth the retrieved level is run at. Against the full corpus at $K = 5$ the retriever places every gold passage a query needs inside the top five for $74$ of the $100$ queries, $41$ of the $50$ direct and $33$ of the $50$ multi-hop; gold recall over all annotated passages is $123/152$, or $80.9\%$. At $K = 10$ the same retriever places every gold passage a query needs inside the top ten for $88$ of the $100$ queries, $45$ of the $50$ direct and $43$ of the $50$ multi-hop, and gold recall rises to $139/152$, or $91.4\%$. Recall cannot fall as the depth grows, so the second set of figures bounds the first from above; what the comparison shows is how much of the shortfall at $K = 5$ is a matter of depth rather than of the retriever failing to rank the passage at all. Fourteen of the $26$ queries that fail the gate at $K = 5$ pass it at $K = 10$, and the multi-hop category accounts for ten of those, which is what the gate's asymmetry predicts: a query needing two passages has two chances to be cut off by a short list. The gap between the categories is a consequence of the gate rather than a difference in difficulty: a direct query fails only if its single gold passage is missed, whereas a multi-hop query fails if any one of its two or more gold passages is missed, and among the $26$ failures at $K = 5$, $15$ retrieved some but not all of the passages they needed. These figures fix the size of the retrieved level at each depth. The $26$ queries that fail the gate at $K = 5$ are excluded at $c^{\text{ret}}$ with $K = 5$ alone, where the absence of the evidence would otherwise be confounded with the prompt; the exclusion is recomputed at $K = 10$ from that depth's own gate, so the denominator is stated per depth and the two are not pooled. The remaining three levels set $C$ directly and so are unaffected, and all $100$ queries are used at each of them.

The query set contains $N_Q = 100$ queries, sampled from MS MARCO so that the reference answer $a_q$ is given by annotation rather than by construction. Two categories are used, fifty queries each. They differ in one property, how far the evidence for an answer is spread across passages, and each is obtained by a documented sampling rule:

\begin{description}
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}}$.] Fifty items whose answer is carried by a single passage marked relevant for the query. One passage suffices, so a failure here is a failure to use evidence that was present. This is the noise-robustness testbed of \citet{chen2024rgb}, and it tests whether the system extracts evidence that is directly available.
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}}$.] Fifty items whose human-written answer draws on two or more of the passages marked relevant for that query. The annotation identifies which passages those are, so the requirement to combine evidence is externally recorded rather than asserted. This is the information-integration testbed, and it tests whether the system aggregates evidence distributed over several passages.
\end{description}

The sample is drawn stratified rather than at random within each category. MS MARCO labels every query with one of five coarse intent types, \textsc{description}, \textsc{entity}, \textsc{location}, \textsc{numeric} and \textsc{person}, and those types are distributed very unevenly: on the rows with a well-formed answer, \textsc{description} accounts for $44\%$ and \textsc{person} for $7\%$. Ten queries are drawn from each of the ten cells that the two categories and the five types define, so the query set is balanced on both axes at once and $N_Q = 5 \times 2 \times 10 = 100$.

The reason for balancing is that the type is wanted as a covariate. An unstratified draw reproduces the natural imbalance, which leaves the rarer types with too few queries to condition on and lets an aggregate figure be carried by the behaviour of one type; a prompt effect that held only on \textsc{description} queries would then be indistinguishable from one that held generally. Balancing makes the type something the analysis can condition on rather than something confounded with the category, and Section~\ref{sec:limits} notes what it costs. That cost is real and is stated here rather than left implicit: the query set is no longer a sample of what people type into a search engine, so any figure averaged over it is an average over the design and not an estimate of behaviour on the natural distribution. It does not touch the effect the thesis reports. Every prompt contrast is taken within a query, so the pairing of Section~\ref{sec:goal} is unaffected by how the queries were selected, and $\tau_j$ is an average of within-query differences whatever the marginal composition of $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ happens to be. What the balancing changes is which marginal quantities are interpretable, not whether the treatment contrast is.

The multi-hop half of the design is drawn from a much smaller pool than the direct half. Only $386$ of the $12{,}467$ well-formed validation rows have two or more passages marked relevant, and the thinnest cell, multi-hop \textsc{person}, holds $27$ candidates for the ten drawn. The multi-hop sample is therefore a substantial fraction of what exists rather than a small draw from a large population, which bounds how much a different seed could change it and is worth recording alongside the seed itself.

Two of the four testbeds of \citet{chen2024rgb} are therefore not represented on the query axis: evidence that contradicts what the model already holds, and evidence that will not support an answer at all. Neither is abandoned. Both are properties of the context rather than of the query, and Section~\ref{sec:conditions} varies the context across four levels independently of the category a query belongs to. The corrupted level sets the retrieved passage against the answer the model is most likely to hold in its parameters, which is the counterfactual condition; the empty level withholds evidence entirely, so that on every query the correct behaviour is to decline, which is the abstention condition. Separating the two axes in this way leaves the query set to vary how the evidence is distributed and the evidence levels to vary how good it is, rather than encoding both on the same axis.

Public datasets carry a risk an invented corpus does not: the generator may have seen the questions and their answers during pretraining. This is why the empty-context quantity $\pi_i$ of Section~\ref{sec:conditions} is a primary control here rather than a formality. The runs of each query under $c^{\varnothing}$, from which $\pi_i$ is computed, are cells of the design already counted in the 37{,}500 generations rather than an additional pass, but they are read off and fixed before the main analysis is carried out. Results are reported over the full query set and again over the subset with $\pi_i = 0$, which is the subset on which retrieval was actually tested. \citet{chen2024rgb} and \citet{longpre2021conflicts} report accuracy without documents for the same reason.

\section{Experimental Conditions}
\label{sec:conditions}

The fifteen prompt conditions are those of Section~\ref{sec:promptreq}. Each of the three requirement sets is realised in five wordings: $S_{b,0}$ through $S_{b,3}$ state the same $\Sigma_b$ in four wordings, and $S_{b,4}$ states a modified set $\Sigma_b'$ in which every obligation is weakened to a request. Section~\ref{sec:procedure} reproduces the three canonical forms and gives their provenance. Each requirement is checkable on an output, in the sense of the verifiable instructions of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}.

What is checked differs by baseline, because the baselines do not ask for the same things, and a condition is held only to what its own prompt states. All three ground the answer in the supplied documents, which is the requirement faithfulness measures. Beyond grounding, $\Sigma_B$ fixes the syntax and density of citations, so adherence is checked by parsing document identifiers and counting them per sentence; $\Sigma_C$ requires that the answer be justified, so adherence is checked by looking for a justification marker. $\Sigma_A$ states nothing beyond grounding, so it carries no rule-checkable requirement of its own: its adherence measure is undefined by construction rather than left out by oversight, and faithfulness carries the whole of what that baseline asks for. Holding every baseline to a single common checklist would score conditions against requirements their prompts never stated, which is why no such checklist is used.

The four evidence levels are all produced by setting $C$ directly rather than by changing the corpus and retrieving again, which is what keeps them compatible with the control described in Section~\ref{sec:goal}: the embedding model, the index, the chunking and the retrieval depth are never touched, and no level is obtained by re-running a modified retriever. Setting $C$ also isolates the effect on generation, since the retrieved passages enter the generator directly in Equation~\eqref{eq:token}, whereas changing $D$ would measure the corruption and the retriever's reaction to it together. Each level is an intervention $\mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik})$ in the sense of Chapter 4.

The first level, written $c^{\text{gold}}$, supplies only the passages the annotation marks as supporting the answer, and so records how the system behaves when the evidence is as good as it can be. The second, $c^{\text{ret}}$, supplies the genuine top-$K$ output of the fixed retriever, whatever that happens to contain on a given query; it is the deployment condition, the only level at which the system is observed as it would actually run, and the only level the depth axis of Section~\ref{sec:goal} applies to, since it is the only one whose context comes from the retriever at all. The third, $c^{\text{corr}}$, takes the gold passages and negates the facts that support the answer, following the entity-substitution method of \citet{longpre2021conflicts} and matching the counterfactual documents of \citet{chen2024rgb}; it sets the retrieved evidence directly against whatever the generator holds in its parameters. The substitution is performed when the context is assembled and never in the index, so the corpus is the same for this level as for every other. The level is constructed for both query categories, since the design is fully crossed, and it is the condition under which evidence is set against whatever the generator holds in its parameters. Items whose annotated answer is a single unambiguous entity admit the cleanest substitution; where an answer is longer or less definite the level functions as severe evidence corruption rather than as a sharp counterfactual. The fourth, $c^{\varnothing}$, supplies no context whatever and therefore isolates parametric knowledge on its own; it is the same cell as the retrieved level taken at depth $K = 0$, which is why the depth axis needs no separate condition at its lower end. None of the four is compared against another to make a claim about evidence quality; each exists so that the prompt contrast can be estimated again under different conditions.

The empty level $c^{\varnothing}$ is the control for what the model already knows. Figure~\ref{fig:causal} shows $\theta$ as a cause of the answer, and without this condition its contribution cannot be separated from that of the retrieved evidence. A query with high $\pi_i$ never tested retrieval. \citet{chen2024rgb} report accuracy without documents alongside accuracy with counterfactual documents for the same reason, and \citet{longpre2021conflicts} show that models often answer from memory when the context contradicts it. Results are therefore reported over the full query set and again restricted to queries with $\pi_i = 0$. Because the queries are drawn from public datasets that the generator may have seen during pretraining, a high $\pi_i$ is expected for part of the query set rather than being the exception, which is why the restricted analysis carries more weight here than it would on an invented corpus.

\section{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

Each part of the standard set out in general terms in Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} is estimated by a named measure, and the scope of that measurement is fixed here before any of them is introduced. The standard is restated formally in Section~\ref{sec:procedure}, together with the cost function that grades an answer against it and the quantity that expresses the effect of a prompt condition, since that is where the analysis needs them.

Every measure used here is a generation-stage measure, and retrieval-stage measures are excluded by design. The reason is structural rather than practical. The intervention studied in this thesis sets the system prompt, and the system prompt enters the model at one place only, namely the generator, which produces the answer once the evidence has already been selected. The retrieval term of the model, as Chapter 4 sets it out, does not take the system prompt as an argument at all, so retrieval lies upstream of the intervention and cannot respond to it. Section~\ref{sec:goal} fixes the embedding model, the index and the chunking in addition, and holds the retrieval depth constant within each level of the depth axis, so the passages returned for a given query are not merely unaffected in expectation but literally the same in all fifteen prompt conditions of a given cell. Measures of ranking quality such as mean reciprocal rank or context precision are therefore constant across conditions by construction; a difference in them would be an arithmetic impossibility rather than a finding, and would point to a fault in the implementation. Retrieval quality is established once, as a property of the fixed pipeline, and thereafter belongs to the experimental control rather than to the results. What the experiment measures is confined to what lies downstream of the intervention: given the same evidence in every condition, what the generator does with it.

Since many different texts can express the same answer, what the standard defines is a set of acceptable answers rather than one ideal string. An answer belongs to that set when it satisfies four requirements at once:

\begin{description}
    \item[Grounded.] It asserts only what the retrieved passages state, introducing nothing from outside them.
    \item[Relevant.] It addresses the question that was asked, and where the evidence will not support an answer it says so rather than producing something off-target.
    \item[Correct.] It agrees with the reference answer supplied by the dataset annotation.
    \item[Obedient.] It satisfies every requirement the system prompt states.
\end{description}

An answer that breaks none of the four is a good answer; one that breaks several is worse than one that breaks a single requirement, and a breach of a requirement that matters in the setting the system is built for weighs more heavily than a breach of one that does not. Section~\ref{sec:procedure} makes that grading precise.

The first three requirements are estimated by the three measures that the survey of \citet{yu2024ragevalsurvey} names as the evaluation targets of the generation component, as distinct from those belonging to retrieval. They are adopted here rather than defined anew, and each is suited to this particular setup for a reason worth stating.

\begin{description}
    \item[Faithfulness, also called groundedness.] Whether the answer is derived exclusively from the retrieved context, so that every claim it makes can be traced to a supplied passage and nothing is introduced from elsewhere. This is the measure the experiment turns on. Every one of the fifteen prompt conditions states a grounding constraint, since all three published baselines ground the answer in the supplied documents; within each baseline four state it in identical terms and the fifth with its modal force weakened, and faithfulness is what reveals whether that constraint is obeyed in practice or merely present in the wording. Because the retrieved evidence is identical across conditions, a change in faithfulness cannot be attributed to a change in what was available to the generator, and hallucination becomes observable as a property of the prompt rather than of the retrieval. Faithfulness estimates the first component of the cost, and is scored in the reference-free manner of \citet{es2023ragas}.

    \item[Answer relevance.] Whether the answer addresses the question that was actually asked, as opposed to a neighbouring one, and whether it does so directly. A system prompt can degrade an answer without making it false. Instructions that stress caution can produce hedging and evasion, instructions that stress completeness can produce padding, and instructions that impose a format can pull the response away from the question. Relevance captures these failures, which faithfulness and correctness both miss, and it is also where abstention is read: under the empty evidence level, where no query has support in the context, an answer that declines and says why is responsive, whereas one that supplies unrelated material is not. Answer relevance estimates the second component of the cost, and is likewise among the reference-free measures of \citet{es2023ragas}.

    \item[Correctness.] Whether the answer agrees with the ground-truth answer recorded in the dataset annotation. This is the end-to-end measure of whether the system was of any use, and it is available here precisely because the query set of Section~\ref{sec:corpus} is drawn from annotated question-answer datasets rather than constructed for the experiment. Correctness and faithfulness come apart under the corrupted evidence level: an answer that repeats a corrupted passage is faithful but wrong, and one that overrides it from memory is right but ungrounded. Reporting both is what makes that distinction visible. Correctness estimates the third component of the cost, and is evaluated as accuracy against the annotation in the sense of \citet{chen2024rgb}.
\end{description}

The fourth requirement is measured separately, because it concerns the prompt rather than the evidence. Instruction adherence is scored as prompt-level strict accuracy in the sense of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}: a length limit is checked by counting tokens and a citation requirement by parsing document identifiers, and an answer counts as adherent only if it satisfies every requirement of the prompt at once. This measure is retained alongside the three above because the question of this thesis is whether a system prompt is followed, and the three generation measures record the consequences of following it without recording the following itself. Adherence estimates the fourth and last component of the cost. The requirement set is the same for the baseline and the three reworded conditions, so adherence is directly comparable across those four; the fifth condition states a modified requirement set and is therefore reported separately.

These four measures are described here at a conceptual level, and deliberately so: what matters for the argument is which failure each one detects and why that failure is informative about the system prompt, not the particular arithmetic by which a score is produced. \citet{yu2024ragevalsurvey} give the taxonomy the three are taken from, defining each as a pairwise relation between the response and something else: relevance between response and query, faithfulness between response and retrieved documents, and correctness between response and reference answer. That account is a taxonomy rather than a formulary, and the operational definitions this thesis computes with, including the decomposition of an answer into atomic claims and the rules by which those claims are aggregated into a score, are those of \citet{es2023ragas}.

Correctness and adherence are computed by program, the first as a comparison against the annotated answer and the second by rule, which is the design principle of the verifiable instructions of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}. Faithfulness and answer relevance are scored by a separate language model, as in \citet{es2023ragas} and \citet{saadfalcon2024ares}.

\subsection{The Judge}

Faithfulness and answer relevance are scored by a separate language model, as \citet{chen2024rgb} do for comparable measures. Since this thesis assumes that language models are sensitive to their instructions, the judge cannot be assumed reliable. Three precautions are taken, following \citet{zheng2023judge}, who document position and verbosity biases in this setting. The judge's agreement with human labelling is measured on about 100 held-out outputs before the main run and reported as Cohen's kappa. Agreement is reported for each condition separately rather than pooled, because the format condition is recognisable from its surface alone and any judge error that varied with condition would bias the estimated effect. The judge's own prompt is fixed, and outputs are shown in random order without condition labels.

\section{Procedure and Analysis}
\label{sec:procedure}

The experiment runs in two stages. Retrieval is executed first, once for every query in the set, and its output is written to disk together with the identifiers and positions of the passages returned. Generation is executed second, reading that stored output rather than calling the retriever again. Every generation is an independent request with caching disabled, and the query, the condition indices, the context string, the output and the number of output tokens are recorded for each one.

The separation is not an implementation convenience. Section~\ref{sec:intervention} assumes that the system prompt has no effect on which documents are retrieved, and running retrieval to completion before any prompt condition is applied is what enforces that assumption rather than merely asserting it. No execution path remains along which a prompt could reach the retriever, because every prompt condition reads the same stored passages.

The retrieval stage is also where the evidence is checked, and it is run once per depth. A query counts as a retrieval success at a given depth when the passages its annotation marks as necessary appear among the $K$ returned at that depth. For a direct query that is the single passage carrying the answer; for a multi-hop query it is every passage the annotated answer draws on, since an answer that requires two passages cannot be produced from one. Success is recorded per query before any output is generated.

Queries whose retrieval failed are excluded from the evaluation of the generation stage at the retrieved evidence level $c^{\text{ret}}$. The measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics} are not defined on evidence that was never supplied. When the context lacks the passage carrying the answer, a correct answer can only have come from parametric memory, which is the behaviour the grounding constraint exists to suppress, and an incorrect answer cannot be told apart from a faithful reading of the distractors. Scoring such a cell would measure the retriever and report the result as an effect of the prompt.

The exclusion is applied uniformly across prompt conditions and separately within each depth. Retrieval is identical in all fifteen conditions at a given depth, so retrieval success is a property of the query and the depth rather than of the condition, and a failed query is dropped from all fifteen at once. The pairing of Section~\ref{sec:goal} therefore survives: every comparison is still made within a query, and no condition is evaluated on a subset that another condition was not.

The gate applies to $c^{\text{ret}}$ alone, at each depth that level is run at. The other three evidence levels of Section~\ref{sec:conditions} set the context directly rather than retrieving it, so retrieval cannot fail in them: $c^{\text{gold}}$ supplies the annotated passages by construction, $c^{\text{corr}}$ supplies their corrupted variants, and $c^{\varnothing}$ supplies nothing by design. Those levels are evaluated over the full query set.

Two consequences follow for reporting. The number of retrieval successes is stated with the results at each depth, since it fixes the denominator of every average taken at $c^{\text{ret}}$, and that denominator is smaller than the one used at the other levels and differs between depths. And retrieval success is reported as a property of the fixed pipeline in the sense of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}: it gates which cells are interpretable, and is not an outcome the prompt is credited or blamed for.

\subsection{Provenance of the Prompt Wordings}

The prompt conditions are not composed from scratch. Their wording is taken from prompts published with retrieval-augmented systems, so that the baseline is a form already in use rather than one written to be improved upon. Three published prompts bear on the requirement set of Section~\ref{sec:conditions}, and each is reproduced below as it appears in its source. They differ in how many requirements they state, which is the property that matters here: a prompt stating two requirements offers little for a change in wording to act on, while one stating ten offers a great deal.

The response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench} states the fewest. It grounds the answer in the supplied documents and asks the question, and does nothing else.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}.},label={lst:minimal}]
Use the following pieces of context to answer the question.
{documents}
Question: {question}
\end{lstlisting}

The instruction of the VANILLA configuration of \citet{gao2023alce} states the most. Beyond grounding, it constrains accuracy, engagement, concision, register, and the syntax and density of citations, and it warns that some supplied results may be irrelevant.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The VANILLA instruction of \citet{gao2023alce}, reproduced from their Table 23.},label={lst:alce}]
Write an accurate, engaging, and concise answer for the given
question using only the provided search results (some of which
might be irrelevant) and cite them properly. Use an unbiased and
journalistic tone. Always cite for any factual claim. When citing
several search results, use [1][2][3]. Cite at least one document
and at most three documents in each sentence. If multiple documents
support the sentence, only cite a minimum sufficient subset of the
documents.
\end{lstlisting}

Between the two sits the instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}, which is the one direct precedent for the decomposition of Equation~\eqref{eq:prompt}. Its authors describe it as consisting of three parts and give a rationale for each: the first directs the model to the retrieved passages, the second asks it to read the question carefully, and the third asks for an answer together with a justification. Writing a prompt as an enumerated set of requirements with a stated purpose per requirement is exactly what $\rho(\Sigma,\varphi)$ formalises, and \citet{mao2024fitrag} report that this form outperforms both a bare instruction and a chain-of-thought variant on the same passages.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The comprehensive instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}.},label={lst:fitrag}]
Refer to the passage below and answer the following question.
Make sure you fully understand the meaning of the question and
passages. Then give the answer and explain why you choose this
answer.
Passages: {passages}
Question: {question}
\end{lstlisting}

Two points follow for the conditions of Section~\ref{sec:conditions}. All three sets are used rather than one at an intermediate density, because the objection each would face alone is answered only by the others: a set stating a single requirement leaves the deontic condition almost nothing to weaken, while a set stating ten risks confounding the wording manipulation with the sheer length of the prompt. Observing the same wording effect across all three separates the effect of wording from the effect of density. And no requirement checked here is an invention of this thesis: the citation constraint is the central requirement of \citet{gao2023alce} and the justification constraint the third part of \citet{mao2024fitrag}, which is why adherence to each can be checked by parsing the output rather than by judgement.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

The empirical evaluation rests on a single collection. Every result reported here is conditional on MS MARCO: on its queries, on the passages it supplies, on the relevance judgements its annotators made, and on the answers they wrote. Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase} argues that the topical spread within that collection is wide, and the argument holds, but spread within one corpus is not replication across several. A prompt effect established here bounds what may be expected elsewhere rather than establishing it.

A third bound follows from how the query set was drawn rather than from the collection. Balancing the sample across the five intent types, as Section~\ref{sec:corpus} describes, makes the type usable as a covariate but detaches the query set from the natural distribution of queries: \textsc{person} and \textsc{location} queries are over-represented relative to what MS MARCO actually contains, and \textsc{description} queries under-represented. The consequence is confined but should be stated. The prompt effect $\tau_j$ is an average of within-query differences and is unaffected, since each query is its own control. Any marginal figure reported over the query set is affected, and is a balanced average over the design rather than an estimate of what the system would do on the queries a deployment would actually receive. Where the two readings differ, it is the first that the research question asks for.

The composition of the collection sets a further bound. MS MARCO records what people type into a web search engine, so its queries are short, largely factoid, and drawn from the domains web search covers. Several of the deployments that motivate retrieval in Section~\ref{sec:usecase}, clinical decision support and legal question answering among them, are absent from it, and the documents those settings retrieve over differ from web passages in length, structure and register. Whether an instruction that governs behaviour on this collection governs it on those is left to future work.

